: 0.2887, "v": 0.64488, "k": "water", "lat": 37.81889, "lon": -107.71478}, {"n": "Cunningham Gulch", "u": 0.59015, "v": 0.64725, "k": "water", "lat": 37.818, "lon": -107.587}, {"n": "Arrastra Gulch", "u": 0.52881, "v": 0.67307, "k": "water", "lat": 37.808, "lon": -107.613}, {"n": "Eureka Gulch", "u": 0.63609, "v": 0.48811, "k": "water", "lat": 37.87966, "lon": -107.56748}, {"n": "Poughkeepsie Gulch", "u": 0.498, "v": 0.26877, "k": "water", "lat": 37.96467, "lon": -107.62606}, {"n": "Silver Lake", "u": 0.54466, "v": 0.71944, "k": "water", "lat": 37.79003, "lon": -107.60629}, {"n": "Lake Emma", "u": 0.52319, "v": 0.42985, "k": "water", "lat": 37.90225, "lon": -107.61537}, {"n": "Highland Mary Lakes", "u": 0.62048, "v": 0.79304, "k": "water", "lat": 37.7615, "lon": -107.57418}, {"n": "Red Mountain Pass", "u": 0.29545, "v": 0.43846, "k": "region", "lat": 37.89888, "lon": -107.71201}, {"n": "Stony Pass", "u": 0.67867, "v": 0.70583, "k": "region", "lat": 37.79528, "lon": -107.5495}, {"n": "Cinnamon Pass", "u": 0.7058, "v": 0.34814, "k": "region", "lat": 37.93388, "lon": -107.53784}, {"n": "American Basin", "u": 0.7517, "v": 0.41404, "k": "region", "lat": 37.90833, "lon": -107.51839}, {"n": "Ironton Park", "u": 0.40093, "v": 0.30519, "k": "region", "lat": 37.95055, "lon": -107.66728}], "tours": [{"id": "veins", "name": "The Veins", "blurb": "The economic sheet at work: lodes in red, tunnels, trams and mills \u2014 Silverton's working country, gulch by gulch.", "keys": [{"lat": 37.81194, "lon": -107.66451, "d": 8, "az": 155, "el": 15, "cap": "Silverton, 9,308 feet, platted 1874 in Bakers Park \u2014 the one flat room in a house of mountains. Greene's smelter fired the same year; by 1905 a mill smoked at the mouth of nearly every gulch on this sheet."}, {"lat": 37.798, "lon": -107.611, "d": 7, "az": 170, "el": 16, "cap": "Arrastra Gulch, named for the mule-dragged Mexican mills of 1871, and the Little Giant \u2014 the San Juans' first lode gold. Above hangs Stoiber's Silver Lake works, mine, mill and aerial tram one machine; the Guggenheims bought it in 1901."}, {"lat": 37.809, "lon": -107.588, "d": 7, "az": 185, "el": 15, "cap": "Cunningham Gulch, claim against claim: Highland Mary, Pride of the West, Green Mountain \u2014 and the Old Hundred, whose Level 7 boarding house of 1904 still clings to the wall of Galena Mountain a half-mile above the road."}, {"lat": 37.87972, "lon": -107.56506, "d": 8, "az": 330, "el": 15, "cap": "Eureka and the Sunnyside, staked 1873 on the Bonita fault \u2014 in time the district's greatest mine. Its mill drew ore by tram from Lake Emma's basin until 1978, when the lake broke into the workings on a Sunday and drowned them, with no one below."}]}, {"id": "caldera", "name": "The Caldera", "blurb": "Crossfade to the areal sheet: the San Juan volcanics as read in 1905 \u2014 and the collapsed volcano nobody could yet see.", "keys": [{"lat": 37.876, "lon": -107.615, "d": 28, "az": 205, "el": 24, "cap": "Pull the layer slider to the areal sheet. Cross, Howe and Ransome drew the San Juan tuff and the Silverton and Potosi volcanic series \u2014 a stratigraphy of catastrophe, read patiently in 1905, with no notion yet that the quadrangle sat in a volcano's fallen roof."}, {"lat": 37.90152, "lon": -107.68911, "d": 9, "az": 140, "el": 15, "cap": "The Red Mountains, roasted orange along the caldera's western ring \u2014 acid steam rotted the rock and left chimneys of nearly solid ore. The Yankee Girl and National Belle mined them from 1882, and the creek below still runs red."}, {"lat": 37.899, "lon": -107.618, "d": 9, "az": 250, "el": 16, "cap": "Burbank traced a 'ring-fault zone' through Bonita Peak in 1933; Lipman's crews closed the circle in the 1970s. The Silverton caldera, some 27 million years old, wears the folio's colours as a bullseye."}, {"lat": 37.91, "lon": -107.59, "d": 11, "az": 225, "el": 16, "cap": "And the veins obey it: Sunnyside, Sound Democrat, Gold Prince ride the fracture swarm off the caldera's north rim. The economic sheet and the areal sheet are one map, read twice."}]}, {"id": "rails", "name": "The Narrow Gauge", "blurb": "The D&RG up the Animas canyon in 1882, then Otto Mears' three little railroads \u2014 over Red Mountain, up the Animas, up Cement Creek.", "keys": [{"lat": 37.782, "lon": -107.663, "d": 8, "az": 10, "el": 15, "cap": "July 1882: the Denver & Rio Grande's three-foot gauge climbed out of the Animas canyon into Bakers Park. Freight that had crossed Stony Pass by mule now rolled downhill \u2014 and the same rails carry the Durango excursions still."}, {"lat": 37.87361, "lon": -107.72534, "d": 8, "az": 25, "el": 16, "cap": "Otto Mears \u2014 toll-road builder, interpreter at the Brunot council \u2014 turned his Rainbow Route into the Silverton Railroad in 1888: around the Chattanooga loop and over the eleven-thousand-foot pass to the Red Mountain mines."}, {"lat": 37.83555, "lon": -107.59423, "d": 8, "az": 60, "el": 15, "cap": "The Silverton Northern, 1896, ran up the valley to Eureka's mill and on, by 1904, to Animas Forks \u2014 four-per-cent grades where his tollroad had gone before. Mears printed his railroad passes on silk."}, {"lat": 37.89027, "lon": -107.65034, "d": 8, "az": 205, "el": 15, "cap": "Up Cement Creek, the Silverton, Gladstone & Northerly, 1899, built to feed the Gold King's mill at the head of the grade. Three little railroads out of one mountain town; none outlived its mines."}]}, {"id": "high", "name": "High and Deep", "blurb": "Animas Forks above eleven thousand feet, the avalanche winters \u2014 and the 1873 Brunot Agreement that took these mountains from the Utes.", "keys": [{"lat": 37.93111, "lon": -107.57145, "d": 6, "az": 205, "el": 15, "cap": "Animas Forks, 11,184 feet \u2014 jail, hotel, newspaper-for-a-season and the Duncan house's bay window, all above timberline. The blizzard of 1884 buried the town for twenty-three days; after that, most wintered below."}, {"lat": 37.793, "lon": -107.5915, "d": 7, "az": 120, "el": 16, "cap": "Avalanche country: the 'slide' has names here. In March 1906 a slide carried away the Shenandoah's boarding house in Cunningham Gulch and a dozen men with it \u2014 one deadly season among many the folio does not mark."}, {"lat": 37.88, "lon": -107.61, "d": 26, "az": 180, "el": 22, "cap": "Say it plainly: these mountains are Nuchu \u2014 Ute \u2014 homeland, guaranteed by treaty in 1868. After gold was struck, the 1873 Brunot Agreement took 3.7 million acres of the San Juans for a promised $25,000 a year; within a decade the Utes were driven from nearly all of Colorado."}, {"lat": 37.79528, "lon": -107.5495, "d": 8, "az": 270, "el": 16, "cap": "Stony Pass, 12,592 feet \u2014 the wagon gate over the Divide that hauled the first machinery in, ore and bullion out, the Rio Grande rising just beyond the crest. The rails below killed it, and the high road went quiet."}]}], "mines": [{"n": "Alabama Mine", "u": 0.49774, "v": 0.31954, "c": "mine"}, {"n": "Anglo-Saxon Mine", "u": 0.36152, "v": 0.52165, "c": "mine"}, {"n": "Anvil Mountain South Mine", "u": 0.35551, "v": 0.66429, "c": "mine"}, {"n": "Aspen Mine", "u": 0.48529, "v": 0.63924, "c": "mine"}, {"n": "Auburn Group Mine", "u": 0.65159, "v": 0.45354, "c": "mine"}, {"n": "Barstow Mine", "u": 0.29094, "v": 0.38182, "c": "mine"}, {"n": "Belcher Mine", "u": 0.34106, "v": 0.70371, "c": "mine"}, {"n": "Big Colorado Mine", "u": 0.44863, "v": 0.49516, "c": "mine"}, {"n": "Big Giant Mine", "u": 0.54689, "v": 0.66433, "c": "mine"}, {"n": "Black Bear Mine", "u": 0.22943, "v": 0.3789, "c": "mine"}, {"n": "Bon Homme Mine", "u": 0.79409, "v": 0.31722, "c": "mine"}, {"n": "Boston Mine", "u": 0.57886, "v": 0.30663, "c": "mine"}, {"n": "Brooklyn Mine", "u": 0.2875, "v": 0.53737, "c": "mine"}, {"n": "Buck Run Mine", "u": 0.45389, "v": 0.39552, "c": "mine"}, {"n": "Buffalo Boy Mine", "u": 0.66814, "v": 0.66858, "c": "mine"}, {"n": "Columbus Mine", "u": 0.62729, "v": 0.35033, "c": "mine"}, {"n": "Delano Mine", "u": 0